\section*{AP \({ }^{\text {® }}\) CALCULUS AB \\ 2011 SCORING GUIDELINES (Form B)}

\section*{Question 6}

Let \(g\) be the piecewise-linear function defined on \([-2 \pi, 4 \pi]\) whose graph is given above, and let \(f(x)=g(x)-\cos \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find \(\int_{-2 \pi}^{4 \pi} f(x) d x\). Show the computations that lead to your answer.
\item[(b)] Find all \(x\)-values in the open interval \((-2 \pi, 4 \pi)\) for which \(f\) has a critical point.
\end{itemize}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/140370d1-b2e2-4ab3-b364-03f3550df8a1-1.jpg?height=436&width=698&top_left_y=402&top_left_x=1233)
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)] Let \(h(x)=\int_{0}^{3 x} g(t) d t\). Find \(h^{\prime}\left(-\frac{\pi}{3}\right)\).
\item[(a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{-2 \pi}^{4 \pi} f(x) d x & =\int_{-2 \pi}^{4 \pi}\left(g(x)-\cos \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)\right) d x \\
& =6 \pi^{2}-\left[2 \sin \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)\right]_{x=-2 \pi}^{x=4 \pi} \\
& =6 \pi^{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[(b)] \(f^{\prime}(x)=g^{\prime}(x)+\frac{1}{2} \sin \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)= \begin{cases}1+\frac{1}{2} \sin \left(\frac{x}{2}\right) & \text { for }-2 \pi<x<0 \\ -\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2} \sin \left(\frac{x}{2}\right) & \text { for } 0<x<4 \pi\end{cases}\)
\(f^{\prime}(x)\) does not exist at \(x=0\).
For \(-2 \pi<x<0, f^{\prime}(x) \neq 0\).
For \(0<x<4 \pi, f^{\prime}(x)=0\) when \(x=\pi\).
\(f\) has critical points at \(x=0\) and \(x=\pi\).
\item[(c)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
& h^{\prime}(x)=g(3 x) \cdot 3 \\
& h^{\prime}\left(-\frac{\pi}{3}\right)=3 g(-\pi)=3 \pi
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
\(2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { antiderivative } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\(4:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \frac{d}{d x}\left(\cos \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)\right) \\ 1: g^{\prime}(x) \\ 1: x=0 \\ 1: x=\pi\end{array}\right.\)
\(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}2: h^{\prime}(x) \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
